#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Builds the demo tracker, renders its board against the tracker's own transcripts, and prints
# where to click. The board reads the sessions from the `Session:` trailer on every commit, so the
# fixture's four sessions are the four that committed on its tickets.
set -euo pipefail

here=$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)
tracker=${1:-/tmp/board-demo}
uv run "$here/../../../../mx/skills/tracker/demo_tracker.py" "$tracker" >/dev/null
CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$tracker/claude" "$here/../../../../mx/bin/board" "$tracker/agent/tickets" --no-watch --no-open

cat <<TEXT

Open the page above and click "Map columns once per bank" in needs me.

Under *sessions on this machine* are the two of its four sessions this machine can name:

    Grilling the CSV import         2026-09-14                  copy resume
    Dispatching csv-import, wave 1  2026-09-17 -> 2026-09-18     copy resume

The worker that built it ran on another host, and the fourth session never touched this ticket.
Hover a button to read the command it copies, and click it:

    cd $tracker && claude --resume a1f3c9e2-4b7d-4e21-9c35-7d2e8f1b6a04
    claude --resume b52e7d10-9a3c-4f68-8e17-3c9b2a5d4e81

The second ran in a feature worktree dispatch has since removed, which is where every locally
dispatched session ends up: no directory to return to, and the resume finds it anyway.

These two sessions are the fixture's invention, so the commands answer "No conversation found with
session ID". Run the board on a tracker of your own for commands that resume.
TEXT
